ork in  the theory of life business  frankly  well it's it's it's not for the faint of  heart that's for sure and you know in in  the book i actually write about you know  some figures in history who  who really tried to go out there to  life's edge the title of the book and  you know that edge where  you're trying to figure out what divides  life from non-life  and they've  they've flamed out they've made terrible  mistakes and they've they've gone down  you know  gone down into obscurity for  for  coming up with crazy ideas that really  just  we're completely off base um you know we  we  we  we tend to think of science as just  nothing but a sort of a series of  triumphs and a  linear progression from  one deep insight to the next and you  know that's not how science works  science is is full of branches in  different directions many of which just  lead to complete failure who is one of  those failures  well i talk in the beginning of the book  about a physicist named john butler  burke who um he was  at cambridge and he was working at this  lab where in the  early 1900s where you know the electron  had just been discovered you know  quantum physics was was being uh  uncovered for the first time in places  like this lab  um  radioactivity was was another thing that  was fascinating people there because it  could tell you about how atoms worked in  this very strange way  and people were fascinated by the  element radium for example and they they  would you know there would be dance  performances where the dancers were  dressed in radium  clothing that glowed in the dark  um and so in this context burke uh  thought well maybe  you know radioactivity is is at the at  the maybe that's what life's about you  know maybe radioactivity is the  organizing principle behind life  and so he ran an experiment where he  took some sterilized beef broth and he  dropped some radium into it and then let  it stay there overnight and then he  went into the lab the next day  and he saw that a layer had formed on  top of the broth and so he scooped some  of that out put it in her  slide looked at it under a microscope  and he convinced himself that he was  seeing um  microbes um teeny tiny structures that  grew and became more complex over time  that seemed to divide  and these were much smaller than  microbes and  but they seemed to be alive to him or at  least to be right on the border and so  he named them radiobs after  radioactivity  published a paper about them wrote a  book about them was just the the talk of  the town i mean people were saying that  burke was important as darwin  and this glory lasted for maybe a year  until some other scientists re-ran the  experiments and showed that it was all  um a fluke from bad chemistry you know  if you use distilled water this just  didn't happen  and that was it and he for the rest of  his life uh  insisted that um  that he uh  uh had been right and that radios really  were there at the at the at that life's  edge  um and uh you know nobody knows who  burke is now um  you know in a way he was a real pioneer  you know he actually used the term  artificial life for the first time for  what he had done  you know trying to make life in a lab he  was trying to do it um but uh but we  don't remember him because he was  playing this very very dangerous game  dangerous status job being a scientist  life's edge the search for what it means  to be alive will be linked in the show  notes below if people want to keep up to  date with what you do where should they  go  uh my website is carlsimmer.com and i'm  carl zimmer on twitter i love it thanks  carl what's happening people thank you  very much for tuning in if you enjoyed  that episode then press here for a  selection of the best clips from the  podcast over the last few weeks and  don't forget to subscribe  peace  you
 

